---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "WebPage",
    name: "Privacy",
    url: "https://tools.ly/privacy/",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Tools.ly does and does not collect: every tool runs in your browser, there are no accounts and no uploads."
  canonical="/privacy/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-head" style="padding-top:2rem">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy, in plain language</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The short version: the tools calculate on your device, there is no account, and nothing you
        type into a tool is transmitted to us.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What we do not collect</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No account, so no name, email address or password is stored about you.</li>
        <li>No upload. Files you select in a media tool are processed in the browser tab and never sent anywhere.</li>
        <li>No profile is built from the values you enter into a calculator.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
      <p>
        Some tools remember things between visits — for example the notepad and the robot game store
        their state in your browser's <code>localStorage</code>. That data never leaves your device,
        it is not visible to us, and clearing your browser's site data removes it permanently.
        Private/incognito windows discard it as soon as you close the window.
      </p>

      <h2>Hosting and server logs</h2>
      <p>
        The site is served as static files by a content delivery network. Like almost every web
        host, the network records standard technical request data (IP address, user agent, requested
        path, timestamp) in order to serve the request and defend against abuse. Those logs are
        controlled by the hosting provider, not by the tools.
      </p>

      <h2>Third parties</h2>
      <p>
        The tool pages deliberately avoid third-party scripts and external fonts, so opening a tool
        does not hand your visit to an advertising or analytics network. Where a page links out to a
        source (for example a standards document), that link goes to the destination you clicked and
        is marked as an external link.
      </p>

      <h2>Health and fitness tools</h2>
      <p>
        The body-composition, calorie and weight tools produce estimates from published formulas for
        information only. They are not medical advice. Measurements you enter stay in the browser
        tab and are used only to produce the numbers you see on screen.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        If anything here is unclear, or you believe a tool is doing something this page does not
        describe, <a href="/7b3c2-contact/">tell us</a> — that is treated as a bug.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
